{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792331942,"nonce":"CSWhJYoLdgl1MXR8YeZE16agkDI=","title":"zypy: functions compile at their twentieth call, so a short run's first iterations wait on compiles","message":"The tiered backend's baseline threshold is 20 interpreted calls (warm at 5, when the worker is asked). A Python program has many small functions that run briefly, and with CI's --fast (5 iterations) on 3- and 4-core runners the first iteration carries those compiles: deltablue's first iteration has about 150 quick baselines and first-call compiles, spectral-norm's main thread waits while about 90 ms of compiles run beside it.\n\nNUC, 4 pinned cores, CI's figure, 7 interleaved reps, 80 against 20 on main 7ac7d368: crypto 0.775, spectral-norm 0.830, chaos 0.906, float 0.911, raytrace 0.950, nbody 0.955, fft 0.959, SOR 0.967, LU 0.976, pyflate 0.978, sparsematmult 0.983, montecarlo 0.984; ai, deltablue, fannkuch, go, hexiom2, json, meteor, nqueens, telco unchanged. 3 cores: crypto 0.81, spectral-norm 0.80, float 0.83, fft 0.85, chaos 0.89, meteor 0.91. richards over 30 reps: 0.984 (see 3485c7a for its bimodal steady state). 200 helps about as much but costs deltablue 8%.\n\nzypy now sets 80; ZYNTAX_BASELINE_THRESHOLD still overrides it. zylua keeps 20 until its benchmarks are measured the same way.","files":null}]}